le raising for value is standard with this hand, the positional disadvantage and the desire to minimize variance in the early game lead me to maintain the check. The goal is to see a cheap flop, assess the board's texture, and react accordingly, maximizing my chances of a favorable outcome.\n\n\n", 'output': "Evaluation: You hold Pocket Nines (9d, 9h), which is a very strong starting hand in heads-up poker (top ~6% of hands). The opponent, in the Small Blind position, has limped (called the Big Blind amount). As the Big Blind, you have the option to Check or Raise. While raising to extract value and deny equity to overcards is a valid aggressive play, checking is a strategic choice here. By checking, you keep the pot small while playing Out of Position (OOP) for the rest of the hand. This disguises the strength of your hand and prevents you from being blown off your equity if the opponent 3-bets (reraises) as a trap. Since this is the first hand of the match and stacks are deep, minimizing variance and avoiding a bloated pot OOP is a sound strategy. You can re-evaluate on the flop; if low cards appear, your hand is disguised and strong; if overcards appear, you haven't committed extra chips.\n\nReason: Checking allows you to see the flop for free, controlling the pot size while playing out of position, and avoids potential pre-flop traps or variance in the first hand.\n\nAnswer: 0"}
