l-in), -1 (Fold), 0 (Check), 100 (raise the bet), or any value above 100 but lower than your current chips.].\n', 'reasoning': '**Analyzing the Texas Hold\'em Situation**\n\nOkay, so I\'m in a heads-up situation in Texas Hold\'em. I\'ve got 19,900 chips left, starting with 20,000, and I\'m looking at my hand: Jack of Diamonds and 7 of Hearts. The community cards are 5d, 4h, Ks, 9c, and finally, 7s. That gives me a pair of sevens, with a King, Jack, and nine as kickers. Not a powerhouse, but not terrible either. It\'s a rainbow board, so no flushes are possible.\n\nConsidering the board texture, I can see some straight possibilities. There is a 6-8 straight and a 3-6 straight. My hand is a mid pair, ranking third among the cards on the board.\n\nThe opponent has been incredibly passive. They\'ve checked every street: pre-flop, flop, turn, and now the river. This could mean they have a very weak hand or a disguised monster. But checking four times screams "showdown value" - something they want to get to the end cheaply, like a weak pair or just air. And after checking the river, they are definitely not bluffing.\n\nThe pot is likely small, given the history, maybe 200-400 chips.  I\'m the one who needs to act now.\n\nI have a few options: check, bet, or go all-in. Fold is not an option here, as I have showdown value with my pair of sevens. Checking seems the most likely, as I am not looking to bluff.\n\nIf I bet, what will I get called by? The only hands that might call are A-high or lower, or smaller pairs. But could I be value-owned by a better seven, a King, or a nine? My sevens might beat Ace-high, and if my opponent shows a five or four, I win. Kx and 9x would be unlikely to fold. Checking might be the best play.\n\nGoing all-in would be a massive overplay for my hand.\n\nGiven the opponent\'s passivity and my hand\'s showdown value, checking seems like the most logical and potentially profitable move. I might win, I might lose, but it\'s the least risky option here. My pair of sevens is a classic "showdown value" hand, good enough to win sometimes, but not strong enough to bet and risk a larger loss. So, I will **check**.\n\n\n', 'output': "Evaluation: The game is at the River stage. The board is 5d, 4h, Ks, 9c, 7s. I have Jd, 7h, giving me a pair of 7s (3rd pair on board) with a Jack kicker. The opponent has checked on every single street (Pre-flop, Flop, Turn, and now River). This extreme passivity suggests the opponent likely has a weak hand, missed draws, or perhaps a low pair. It is unlikely they hold a King or 9, as they likely would have bet earlier to extract value or protect their hand.\n\nMy pair of 7s has decent showdown value. Betting here is risky because better hands (like Kx, 9x, or better 7x) will likely call, and worse hands (like Ace-high or missed straights) will likely fold, meaning I don't extract much value from worse hands and only lose to better ones. Checking allows me to realize my equity for free. If my hand is best, I win the pot. If not, I lose nothing further.\n\nReason: My hand (Pair of 7s) has showdown value but is not strong enough to value bet with confidence on the river, given the board texture. The opponent's passive line suggests I might have the best hand, so checking back to see the showdown is the optimal low-variance play.\n\nAnswer: 0"}
