ue above 100 but lower than your current chips.].\n', 'reasoning': '**Analyzing the River Decision**\n\nOkay, so it\'s hand 12 of 20, and I\'m up +550.  We\'ve got 20,000 chips each to start, now at 19,700. My hole cards are 6h, 8h. The board is 3s, 8c, 8s, 10s, 4h. I\'ve got trips with an 8-6-6-10 kicker. The board is scary with three spades out there, but I\'m holding a strong hand.  Pre-flop, I\'m pretty sure I limped, and the opponent likely checked. On the flop and turn, it went check-check and then I bet 200, which the opponent called. Now it\'s the River, and the opponent has been passive so far.\n\nThe pot\'s probably around 400-600 chips now.  My options are to check, fold, go all-in, or bet.  Since I have trips, I want to get some value, but I need to be careful of a flush or full house. The prompt suggests a valid "bet" of at least 100.  The opponent\'s previous call on the turn suggests a decent hand, possibly a 10, or a flush draw that didn\'t hit. I definitely don\'t want to get blown out on this turn, it is a very dangerous board. I don\'t think they have a full house because they likely would have raised with such a strong hand. A flush is possible, but they\'d likely have bet if that hit.\n\nMy gut says bet small.  I\'m thinking 200, the same as the turn bet. It\'s a pot-sized bet that is small and likely to induce a call from worse hands like a 10 or a lower pair that didn\'t improve. If they raise, it\'s a tough decision, but given their previous passivity, I\'ll assume a made hand.  Checking is safe, but I want to get some value from them while protecting the small lead I have. Given the valid actions prompt "100" and above, I will open and bet 200 for now. Hopefully, they will call and I will be able to maintain my lead and increase my chips in a safe manner.\n\n**My Decision:** Bet 200\n\n\n', 'output': "Evaluation: The player holds Three of a Kind (Trip 8s) with a 6 kicker. The board is paired (8s) and has three spades (3s, 8s, 10s), presentin